hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about the wild feelings test the wild  Felix test was developed over 100 years  ago by two scientists who realized that  they could use a technique that they had  developed as a diagnostic test for  Rickettsia infections we say Rickettsia  infections we're talking about things  like typhus and Rocky Mountain spotted  fever so infections caused by  rickettsial bacteria it's a simple and  inexpensive test it is less sensitive  and some newer methods like  immunofluorescence antibody assays but  because it is so simple and inexpensive  to carry out it is still pretty widely  used so let's talk about the mechanism  how it works it turns out that there is  antigenic cross reactivity between  Rickettsia species and some Proteus  species I'll talk about what this means  so Rickettsia is one genus of bacteria  that causes diseases like the ones I  mentioned earlier typhus Rocky Mountain  spotted fever Proteus is another genus  of bacteria but it turns out there's  antigenic cross reactivity what this  means is that antibodies that were made  by an immune system to recognize and  fight Rickettsia species can also  recognize and bind to some Proteus  species and so what medical  professionals can do is they can make  delusions of patient serum so the serum  being the portion of the blood that  contains the antibodies and test those  against different Proteus strains  looking for this nonspecific  agglutination  the nonspecific here just refers to the  fact that they're looking to see if  there are antibodies that would be have  been made by an immune system to  recognize for Casilla species that can  actually recognize the Proteus species  in the test resulting in this  cross-linking that you see here so each  of these black rods represents a Proteus  bacterium and the red spikes coming off  each of the Proteus bacteria those are  to represent antigens antigens just  meaning something usually like a  carbohydrate or a protein on the surface  of a bacterium that can be recognized by  the antibodies there here in green  agglutination is when you get clumping  so when you get lots of these Proteus  bacteria that are cross-linked meaning  that you've got an antibody that is  bound to two Proteus bacteria bringing  them into close sort of close contact  with each other so you have all of this  cross-linking these different antibodies  again those are the Y shaped molecules  shown in green that causes clumping of  these Proteus bacteria and the clumping  is something that can actually be seen  with the naked eye and that's called  agglutination  so just to kind of summarize if somebody  is sick because they're infected with  Rickettsia all species and their body is  making antibodies to try to defend them  trying to fight off those infections  well those antibodies will be in the  patient serum and a doctor can take the  serum mix it with some Proteus and see  if agglutination happens if the Proteus  is agglutinated then the doctor can can  determine that the patient has  antibodies in their serum to Rickettsia  infections which would mean that they  were infected with something  titanosaur Rocky Mountain spotted fever  the bacteria that caused us so there are  two main methods that can be used for  the wild Felix test that's the slide  test and the tube test the flight test  is the simplest but it's not very  quantitative it involves putting patient  serum onto a slide and then adding drops  of a Proteus bacterial culture and then  rotating for one minute so that just  means rotating the slide like holding  the slide and kind of rotating it like  this just so that the serum and the drop  of the previous culture get well mixed  and then you look if the serum is  positive meaning that it has antibodies  that have been made against the  rickettsial infection those antibodies  will also non specifically cross-linked  the Proteus resulting in agglutination  and you'll see sort of some cloudy  clumping in the serum if the serum from  the patient does not have a gluten ation  that it's negative for these antibodies  meaning that that patient would not have  a Rickettsia infection so that's the  slide test the next is the two tests it  takes longer but it's more quantitative  this is where a doctor will make  delusions of the patient serum so here  you would have from the most  concentrated to least concentrated  meaning the most antibodies are going to  be in here the least antibodies are  going to be in here and you'll see a  spectrum because of a serial dilution to  each one of these tubes you would add a  drop of Proteus culture and incubate for  a few hours four to six hours at fifty  to sixty degrees Celsius and then check  for the agglutination the clumping the  most dilute too so that basically the  most eluc to mean the least concentrated  tube that shows the agglutination gives  the answer  so if you look and this is the most  concentrated and it's got agglutination  and this is the second most concentrated  and it's got a glutton nation but  there's no agglutination here then this  tube right here would be the most dilute  tube to show the agglutination and you  would look back at what the dilution was  and that would help you to determine the  antibody titer I do want to point out  one more thing and that is that  antibodies to different species of  Rickettsia will be cross reactive with  different species of Proteus which means  that you might have to do these tests a  few different times using different  species or strains of Proteus in order  to determine which kind of Rickettsia  infection somebody has if any so - to  screen them for the different types of  repetitive infections you might have to  go through this process a few different  times if you're interested in learning  more about how antibodies work then you  can check out my video on antibody  structure and function if you're  interested in how these antibodies can  be used to test for other kinds of  infections your the well Felix test is  looking at rickettsial infections but  I've got another video on the Sabin  Feldman diet test which looks at  antibodies and uses them to determine if  a person has a Toxoplasma infection so  you can check out that video if you're  interested in that but that's it for  today and thanks for watching biology  professor
 

